<<
/Title <>
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 824 0 R
/Next 851 0 R
>>
endobj
851 0 obj
<<
/Title (The researcher-recorded interview notes were reviewed as case-level accounts. Analysis followed phases informed by Braun and Clarke: familiarisation with the data; generation of initial codes; construction of candidate themes; review of themes against the dataset; definition and naming of themes; and preparation of the analysis in dialogue with the literature. The unit of coding was a meaningful account of an event, decision, or relationship. Initial codes included self-advocacy, earned promotion, uncertainty during role transitions, preparation, sponsor access, visibility, unequal recognition, care priorities, work boundaries, restorative routines, mentoring, psychological strain, perceived bias, and stakeholder navigation. Accounts that challenged a dominant interpretation were retained, including those of participants who reported little or no self-doubt. The six themes are researcher-generated interpretations. The study does not claim independent dual coding, participant validation, saturation, or formal inter-rater reliability; such claims would require supporting documentation.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 824 0 R
/Prev 850 0 R
>>
endobj
852 0 obj
<<
